"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,625\nThe Learning Brain. Series 4: Habits\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,391\nEpisode 5\n\n3\n00:00:07,471 --> 00:00:10,456\nFood, caffeine and the glucose myth\n\n4\n00:00:13,256 --> 00:00:16,037\nExam week often brings sugary snacks,\n\n5\n00:00:16,271 --> 00:00:18,389\nenergy drinks, and skipped meals.\n\n6\n00:00:18,783 --> 00:00:22,189\nSo what does the evidence say about fuelling your brain to study?\n\n7\n00:00:22,359 --> 00:00:25,130\nYour brain is about two percent of your body weight,\n\n8\n00:00:25,518 --> 00:00:28,759\nbut it uses roughly twenty percent of your energy at rest.\n\n9\n00:00:29,276 --> 00:00:31,797\nHard thinking adds only a little on top,\n\n10\n00:00:31,895 --> 00:00:33,729\nperhaps around five percent.\n\n11\n00:00:34,231 --> 00:00:38,082\nYour brain is not running out of fuel halfway through a problem set.\n\n12\n00:00:38,252 --> 00:00:42,318\nA popular idea held that self control burns blood sugar,\n\n13\n00:00:42,638 --> 00:00:44,677\nand a sugary drink tops it back up.\n\n14\n00:00:45,276 --> 00:00:50,583\nBut a 2016 analysis found signs of publication bias in that research,\n\n15\n00:00:50,833 --> 00:00:52,606\nand weak evidence overall.\n\n16\n00:00:53,168 --> 00:00:58,195\nA later experiment with three hundred and seventy one people found no meaningful link.\n\n17\n00:00:58,365 --> 00:00:59,557\nMeals still matter.\n\n18\n00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:04,672\nA review of forty five studies in children and teenagers found that breakfast,\n\n19\n00:01:04,718 --> 00:01:08,946\ncompared with fasting, gave a short term boost that same morning,\n\n20\n00:01:09,202 --> 00:01:11,261\nespecially for attention, memory,\n\n21\n00:01:11,281 --> 00:01:12,665\nand planning tasks.\n\n22\n00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:15,628\nIt could not say which breakfast works best.\n\n23\n00:01:15,798 --> 00:01:18,208\nCaffeine is the most common study aid.\n\n24\n00:01:18,770 --> 00:01:20,659\nLow to moderate doses,\n\n25\n00:01:20,895 --> 00:01:23,179\nroughly forty to three hundred milligrams,\n\n26\n00:01:23,347 --> 00:01:26,800\nimprove alertness, attention, and reaction time.\n\n27\n00:01:27,322 --> 00:01:31,268\nEffects on memory and complex reasoning are less consistent.\n\n28\n00:01:31,438 --> 00:01:32,613\nThe catch is sleep.\n\n29\n00:01:33,142 --> 00:01:40,146\nIn one study, four hundred milligrams of caffeine taken six hours before bed cut sleep by more than an hour,\n\n30\n00:01:40,397 --> 00:01:42,127\nand people did not notice.\n\n31\n00:01:42,297 --> 00:01:43,266\nSome caution.\n\n32\n00:01:43,658 --> 00:01:46,166\nThe breakfast studies are mostly in children.\n\n33\n00:01:46,565 --> 00:01:49,699\nThe caffeine and sleep study had just twelve people.\n\n34\n00:01:50,007 --> 00:01:53,922\nAnd responses to caffeine vary a lot from person to person.\n\n35\n00:01:54,092 --> 00:01:54,900\nTry this.\n\n36\n00:01:55,219 --> 00:01:56,471\nEat at regular times,\n\n37\n00:01:56,491 --> 00:01:58,520\nespecially before morning exams.\n\n38\n00:01:58,838 --> 00:02:01,421\nDon't count on sugar for a willpower boost.\n\n39\n00:02:01,787 --> 00:02:03,792\nUse caffeine early in the day,\n\n40\n00:02:03,839 --> 00:02:05,454\nfor alertness, not memory.\n\n41\n00:02:05,769 --> 00:02:09,081\nAnd stop caffeine at least six hours before bed.\n\n42\n00:02:09,251 --> 00:02:11,189\nFeed your routine, not the myths.\n\n"
